cd /hutian/repo/paper; cat > sections/results.tex <<'E'
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

All results are for $n=100$ unless a figure varies $n$; ratios are RMSE relative to uniform sampling with the sample mean, so a value below $1$ is better than uniform.
Table~\ref{tab:primary} gives the primary endpoint, with the number of targets and families behind each column.
Names of cells that are the best of the grid were chosen after the outcomes and are marked as such; the intervals of those cells are optimistic.

\subsection{Which methods beat uniform sampling, and where?}
\label{sec:res-compare}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\small
\caption{Primary endpoint: RMSE relative to uniform~$\times$~mean at $n=100$ items, geometric mean over benchmarks with a 95\% bootstrap interval over target families (non-thin benchmarks). Rows 1--7: one fixed representative per method class; rows 8--10: the best methods by this endpoint, chosen after seeing the results. The lower block takes stratified~$\times$~mean as the baseline. The last rows count benchmarks, targets and target families. Benchmarks are not independent; the intervals reflect target-family sampling only.}
\label{tab:primary}
\input{../tables/table2_primary}
\end{table}

Three method classes behave differently as the targets move away from the sources (Table~\ref{tab:primary}, Figure~\ref{fig:ratio}).
\emph{Model-based predictors} that estimate the target from a latent model fitted on the sources (IRT model, kNN, ridge) halve the error of uniform sampling at small $n$ when targets are interpolated, but their advantage shrinks as $n$ grows, and for targets above every source they are worse than uniform: $1.96$ $[1.82,2.04]$ for the IRT model, $2.42$ for kNN and $1.47$ for ridge, rising to about $3.8$ for the IRT model at $n=400$.
\emph{Anchor and information-selected subsets} do not beat uniform at $n=100$ in interpolation or lineage ($0.95$ and $1.03$ for anchors with IRT plug-in) and lose heavily in extrapolation ($1.83$); deterministic information-top selection with the sample mean is $2.99$ in extrapolation and above $3$ in the other two splits.
\emph{Methods that keep a probability design or correct for the residual} (stratified or balanced sampling, calibrated regression, the difference estimator) are below $1$ in every split, except calibrated regression in extrapolation ($1.04$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/fig2_ratio_vs_n}
\caption{RMSE relative to uniform sampling (below 1 is better) against the number of items $n$, one panel per target split; log-log axes. Lines: geometric mean over non-thin benchmarks (13; 12 for lineage); bands: 95\% family-bootstrap interval. One representative per method class plus the three best methods by the primary endpoint (chosen after the results).}
\label{fig:ratio}
\end{figure}

How much of the gain comes from the sources?
Stratified sampling, which uses only the sources' item difficulties to order items, already gives $0.81$, $0.85$ and $0.83$.
Against it, the best difference-corrected cells keep a gain of $0.80$ $[0.78,0.82]$ in interpolation and $0.84$ in lineage, and in extrapolation $0.91$ $[0.90,0.93]$ (balanced $\times$ calibrated regression) or $0.96$ $[0.93,0.99]$ (balanced $\times$ difference IRT); for uniform design with the difference IRT estimator the interval includes $1$ ($1.01$ $[0.98,1.03]$).
Over the plain stratified sample, the sources therefore buy about a fifth of the error when targets resemble them and under a tenth when every target is stronger than every source.
The extrapolation result also depends on how far above the sources the targets lie: with the top 10\% of models as targets the best difference cell reaches $0.70$ $[0.68,0.72]$, with the top 50\% it is $0.96$ $[0.92,0.98]$ and the interval of uniform $\times$ difference IRT includes $1$ ($0.99$ $[0.96,1.01]$).

